---
import VoiceLimit from '../../components/demos/VoiceLimit.tsx';
import ConcurrencyDiagram from '../../components/diagrams/ConcurrencyDiagram.astro';
import Frame from '../../components/diagrams/Frame.astro';
import Snippet from '../../components/Snippet.astro';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';

const declare = `createEngine({
  buses: {
    sfx: { concurrency: { max: 32, steal: 'oldest' } },
    voice: { concurrency: { max: 1, steal: 'lowest-priority' } },
  },
});`;

const live = `engine.bus('sfx').setConcurrency({ max: 8, steal: 'lowest-priority' });`;

const priority = `// Hero hit — protected from stealing.
engine.sound('player-hit').play({ priority: 10 });

// Footstep — cheap, expendable.
engine.sound('footstep').play({ priority: 0 });`;
---
<Docs
  title="Concurrency"
  description="Voice limits with a steal strategy. Keeps polyphony bounded, and decides which voice dies when the limit is hit."
  eyebrow="Concept"
>
  <p>
    Hold a button that fires a 50 ms click every frame. Thirty seconds later
    you have eighteen hundred live voices, a stalled audio thread, and a fan
    at full tilt. Nothing in the Web Audio API stops you.
  </p>
  <p>
    Concurrency is a per-bus cap on how many voices may sound at once,
    together with a rule for what happens when a new voice arrives at a full
    bus. That rule is the <em>steal strategy</em>. It picks an existing voice
    to end so the new one can play, and it runs synchronously, before
    <code>play()</code> returns.
  </p>

  <h2>How many voices can play at once?</h2>
  <p>
    As many as you allow. The cap is declared per bus, so a UI bus can hold
    four while an SFX bus holds thirty-two. A bus with no
    <code>concurrency</code> block is unbounded, which is the right default
    for music and the wrong one for anything a player can spam.
  </p>

  <Frame caption="Slots are bounded. When full, the steal strategy picks a victim, synchronously, before play() returns.">
    <ConcurrencyDiagram />
  </Frame>

  <h2>Strategies</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>oldest</strong> kills the voice that started first. This is the default, and the right call for SFX rain.</li>
    <li><strong>lowest-priority</strong> kills the voice with the smallest <code>priority</code>. Use it when you have a hierarchy: a hero attack outranks a footstep, which outranks an ambience tick.</li>
    <li><strong>quietest</strong> kills the least audible voice. It ranks candidates by an existing meter tap where one exists and by the voice's own gain otherwise, so it costs nothing per candidate.</li>
    <li><strong>none</strong> rejects the new voice. The returned <a href="/api/Voice/"><code>Voice</code></a> is already ended.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>API</h2>
  <Snippet code={declare} lang="ts" title="At construction" />
  <Snippet code={live} lang="ts" title="Live tuning" />

  <h2>Live demo</h2>
  <p>
    Change <code>max</code> and <code>steal</code>, then mash "Fire voice".
    Slots fill, and once you pass the cap you can watch which voice the
    strategy picks.
  </p>
  <VoiceLimit client:visible />

  <h2>Recipes</h2>

  <h3>Protect important voices</h3>
  <Snippet code={priority} lang="ts" />
  <p>
    With <code>steal: 'lowest-priority'</code>, the player-hit voice survives
    until it ends naturally or another priority-10 voice arrives.
  </p>

  <h2>Pitfalls</h2>
  <div class="pitfall">
    <div class="pitfall-title">Don't use <code>steal: 'none'</code> for SFX.</div>
    Rejecting voices makes a player notice missing sounds in exactly the dense
    moments where they are paying attention. Stealing one is far less
    obvious. Reserve <code>'none'</code> for a voice or alarm bus, where you
    want the announcement that is already playing to finish.
  </div>
  <div class="pitfall">
    <div class="pitfall-title">Don't set max too low for sprites.</div>
    A cascading match-3 can fire twelve or more one-shots in a single frame.
    At <code>max: 4</code> that sounds chopped. Profile real gameplay before
    clamping.
  </div>

  <h2>Related</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/concepts/voice/">Voice</a> carries the <code>priority</code> you set on play.</li>
    <li><a href="/concepts/bus/">Bus</a> is where the concurrency block lives.</li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
